@q 18:29-32

**Sex ratio** is most favourable in the Baltic states and much of Europe (Finland among the options); in 2011 the order was **Bangladesh > Pakistan > India > China**. Sex-determination tests threaten the world sex ratio. By religion, **Christians > Muslims > Hindus > Buddhists**.

@section 27.4 | World Urbanization

@q 18:33-38

@qnote 18:38 | The UN said the world became **half urban in 2008** (UNFPA, *State of World Population 2007*); 2008 is not among the options, so the source marks it (*).

@table
World urbanization fact | Figure
Urban share | **55% (2018)**, about **57-58%** now; **68-70% by 2050**
Urbanization curve | **Initial → acceleration (second stage) → terminal**
UN-Habitat city prosperity | **Productivity, infrastructure, quality of life, equity, environment**; not "optimum population"
Largest urban population | **China**
Most urbanized continent | **Australia-Oceania** (Australia/New Zealand 86%), then North America
Most urbanized region (options) | **Europe**
Most urbanized countries | **Singapore**, Monaco, Kuwait (100%); in South America **Uruguay**; in West Asia **Kuwait**
@end

@q 18:39-48

@qnote 18:42 | When asked (2002) the **USA**'s population exceeded India's urban population (d). Today India's **urban population** (over 50 crore) is larger than the total population of every listed country, so the source marks it (*).

@qnote 18:46 | **Pakistan** was the most urbanized of the options when asked. The source keys **(b) Bhutan**, about 43% urban in 2024 (Pakistan 39%, India 35-37%, Sri Lanka 19%).

Among the South Asian options the order in Q45 was **Pakistan > India > Bangladesh > Sri Lanka**. **Tokyo-Yokohama** is the world's largest urban agglomeration (about 37 million), though UN 2025 estimates now put **Jakarta** and **Dhaka** close behind or ahead.

@q 18:49-51

Urbanization usually **follows industrialisation**; in developing countries it also moves on its own (push from villages), so the Reason is true but not the explanation. Asian cities are **over-urbanized** because their growth has outpaced economic development. Western cities have more **women** because farm work, now mechanised, draws men to the countryside.

@section 27.5 | Migration

@q 18:52-54

@qnote 18:53 | When asked (1999), **Australia** (a) was the answer the source accepts for then. The source keys **(c) UAE**, where about **88%** of residents are migrants (World Migration Report 2024).

About **3.6%** of the world's people (about **304 million**, 2024) live outside their country of birth. **Europe** hosts the most migrants, then Asia; **India** is the largest **country of origin** (about 18 million emigrants).

